## .NET 8 Migration: `EventProcessor`

### Critical Issues in the Original Code

1. **`Thread.Abort()`** — throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 8 (removed since .NET 5)
2. **`Thread.Interrupt()`** — still exists but is obsolete (`SYSLIB0006` in some contexts) and unreliable for clean shutdown
3. **Manual `Thread` + `ManualResetEvent` + `lock`** — replaced idiomatically by `Channel<T>` or `BlockingCollection<T>`
4. **Race condition** — multiple `Enqueue` calls between `WaitOne`/`Reset` lose signals; only one item dequeued per signal

### Migrated Code

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _processingTask;

    public void Start()
    {
        _processingTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        try
        {
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(ct))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // Clean shutdown requested
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        if (!_channel.Writer.TryWrite(item))
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Failed to enqueue item.");
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();           // Signal no more items
        _cts.CancelAfter(timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)); // Hard deadline

        if (_processingTask is not null)
            await _processingTask;
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item)
    {
        // your processing logic
    }
}
```

### Key Migration Pitfalls

| .NET Framework Pattern | Problem in .NET 8 | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `PlatformNotSupportedException` | Use `CancellationToken` for cooperative cancellation |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Unreliable, throws in unexpected places | `Channel.Writer.Complete()` + cancellation token |
| `ManualResetEvent` + `lock` + `Queue` | Race condition (lost signals), manual synchronization | `Channel<T>` handles all synchronization internally |
| `while(true)` + `WaitOne` | No clean exit path | `ReadAllAsync()` completes when channel is closed |
| Fire-and-forget `Thread` | No error observability | `Task` surfaces exceptions |

### If You Need a Synchronous API

Use `BlockingCollection<T>` instead of `Channel<T>`:

```csharp
private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();

// Producer
_queue.Add(item);

// Consumer loop
foreach (var item in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable(ct))
    Handle(item);

// Stop
_queue.CompleteAdding();
```

### Checklist

- ✅ Remove all `Thread.Abort()` / `Thread.Interrupt()` calls
- ✅ Replace `ManualResetEvent` signaling with `Channel<T>` or `BlockingCollection<T>`
- ✅ Use `CancellationToken` for cooperative shutdown
- ✅ Implement `IAsyncDisposable` for resource cleanup
- ✅ Drain remaining items on shutdown (channel reader loop exits after `Complete()` once empty)
- ✅ Fix the lost-signal race condition from the original code
